          "tool_name": "forensic_metascience__recalc_stat_to_p",
          "description": "Recompute a p-value from ONE printed test statistic: t, chi2, z, r or F. Use it wherever a statistic and its p sit in a TABLE or in prose statcheck does not parse -- statcheck verifies these same five distributions, but only as a text scanner over APA grammar, so the same numbers in a table cell were invisible to the whole kit. `recalc_f_to_p` was the only value-level sibling. MEASURED: three archived papers hit this, and on one it downgraded the HEADLINE finding from `impossible` to `highly_suspicious` -- the review wrote \"No tool in this kit converts a chi-squared statistic plus df to a p-value\". IF THE PAPER DOES NOT PRINT THE INGREDIENT, this refuses and NAMES it. A chi-squared without degrees of freedom cannot be converted by any implementation: report that as an incompletely reported test -- a property of the publication -- not as a limit of this toolkit. ROUNDED STATISTIC, so it implies a p RANGE: `chi2 = 4.1` asserts [4.05, 4.15) and the reported p is judged against the whole achievable interval. DIRECTIONAL and capped at `suspicious`: only an OVER-claim is flagged (a p below what the statistic supports). An under-claim is conservative reporting or a threshold. A one-tailed test or a correction would explain a disagreement, so this never accuses. `tails` is ignored for chi2 and F, whose p is an upper tail by construction -- but AT df = 1 that upper-tail p IS the two-tailed p of the underlying t or z (F(1,k) = t(k)^2, chi2(1) = z^2), so half of it is the one-tailed p and a directional hypothesis explains a reported p that is half what you compute. DO NOT write that a one-tailed reading is impossible for a 1-df F or chi2; it is not, and doing so produced a false accusation. Parameters: statistic (float), kind (str), df, df2, n, reported_p, decimals_stat, decimals_p, tailsPASS `sampling='complex_survey'` WHEN THE PAPER SAYS SO -- survey or sampling weights, a complex survey design, NHANES WTMEC*/SDMVPSU, R's survey package, Stata svy:, PROC SURVEY*. This check then returns `indeterminate` inst… [truncated]",
