        {
          "tool_name": "forensic_metascience__extract_figures",
          "description": "Locate every figure in a PDF, crop it, and pair it with its caption. Runs LOCALLY via PyMuPDF -- no network, no API, no token cost. Use it when a paper's outcome appears only in a chart, which is common: the group means and SD/SEM needed to compute an effect size often appear nowhere in the text or tables. Uses CAPTION-ANCHORED cropping rather than embedded-image extraction, because most journal charts are VECTOR linework that `get_images()` does not see at all. Returns per figure: label, page, caption, crop path, `crop_confidence`, and `error_bar_meaning` read out of the caption (SD | SEM | CI | IQR | range, or null). A null there matters -- SD, SEM and a 95% CI half-width differ by sqrt(n) and ~1.96*sqrt(n), so a figure whose error bars are unlabelled cannot yield an effect size at all, and the tool says so instead of assuming SEM. ALWAYS CHECK `crop_confidence` BEFORE READING AN IMAGE. `high` = the artwork was located and cropped to. `partial` = the figure continues past the crop, walled off by a paragraph or another caption, so anything you read is from an INCOMPLETE view of it. `low` = a crop was made but holds too little ink to be a chart, so it is probably a header sliver -- read the page image named in the figure's `warnings` instead. `none` = no artwork was found near the caption and `image_path` IS THE WHOLE PAGE, not the figure; this happens on papers that collect their figure legends onto a separate page, and on captions whose artwork sits on the adjacent page (a known limitation). Reporting a value read from a `low` or `none` image without saying so is a fabricated measurement. Anything you subsequently READ OFF THE MARKS is tier-2 `figure_pixels` provenance and is barred from GRIM, GRIMMER, SPRITE and DEBIT. Caption TEXT is printed and carries normal provenance. Parameters: pdf_path, out_dir (optional), dpi (default 150), render_pages",
