        {
          "tool_name": "forensic_metascience__check_estimate_ci",
          "description": "Reconcile a point estimate against its own confidence interval: interval ordered and containing the estimate, estimate at the CI-implied midpoint ON THE CORRECT SCALE, and the p recovered from the CI agreeing with the reported p. PASS `measure` -- for ratio measures (OR/RR/HR/IRR) the CI is symmetric in LOG space, and checking linear symmetry misfires on 100% of hazard ratios at SE_log = 0.4 (measured, 2000/2000; the scale inference is what keeps the unlabelled path clean). A 'wrong' p is tested against alternative confidence levels before being flagged. Parameters: estimate, ci_low, ci_high, measure (optional), reported_p (optional), reported_p_operator (default \"=\"), confidence_level (default 0.95), null_value (optional; defaults to 1 for ratios, 0 otherwise), decimals (optional). A THRESHOLD p IS EXPRESSIBLE AND MUST BE PASSED AS ONE: for 'P < .001' pass reported_p=0.001 with reported_p_operator='<' ('<=', '>', '>=' likewise for 'P ≤ .05' or 'P > .99'). Never coerce 'P < .001' to reported_p=0.001 alone -- that asserts equality and can be rescued as a mislabelled CI level -- and never omit the p because it is a threshold: that silently skips the p cross-check and records the row as consistent (measured: an abstract-headline 'ARR 0.185 (0.046-0.748), P < .001' whose CI-implied p is .018 passed clean under both encodings). PASS `decimals` -- the PRINTED precision of the COARSEST of the three numbers, since a comparison among them is only known to that precision. Without it the tolerance is re-derived from the parsed floats, and it errs WIDE: a bound printed 1.40 reads as one decimal, because str(1.40) is '1.4', so the tolerance comes out ten times too LOOSE and a real discrepancy can be waved through. An integral 5 reads at zero decimals, which is what it asserts.PASS `sampling='complex_survey'` WHEN THE PAPER SAYS SO (survey weights, NHANES WTMEC*, R's survey package, Stata svy:, PROC SURVEY*). The p-vs-CI comparison then becomes ONE-SID