        {
          "tool_name": "forensic_metascience__recalc_chi_squared",
          "description": "Recalculate chi-squared test from contingency table. Parameters: observed (2D list), reported_chi2 (optional), reported_p (optional), use_yates (default false), decimals_p (optional).PASS `decimals_p` -- a p printed '.03' asserts [.025, .035); one printed '.0312' asserts [.03115, .03125). Without it the comparison uses a fixed two-decimal window plus a relative band that at p = .04 accepts a computed .06, a different significance decision. IS THE TABLE PAIRED? A McNemar test -- the same subjects measured twice -- uses (b-c)^2/(b+c) on the discordant cells, NOT a chi-squared of independence, and the two designs produce identical-looking counts. Pass paired=true when the paper says McNemar (or reports a pre/post agreement table). You do not have to: McNemar is computed on every 2x2 and tried in the same both-conventions match, so a statistic only it explains comes back consistent rather than flagged. `paired` decides which test is the HEADLINE calculated_* value. AND CHECK THE TABLE YOU BUILT. The measured failure here was a 4-category gaze breakdown flattened into a 2x2 that was not the paper's table at all -- no guard in this tool can detect that, and a mismatch against a table the paper never printed is an accusation about your own reconstruction.PASS `sampling='complex_survey'` WHEN THE PAPER SAYS SO -- survey or sampling weights, a complex survey design, NHANES WTMEC*/SDMVPSU, R's survey package, Stata svy:, PROC SURVEY*. This check then returns `indeterminate` instead of running, because a design-based estimate and its standard error are not functions of the printed means, SDs or counts: weighting moves the composition of the sample, not just its variance, so a disagreement is expected in BOTH directions and means nothing. MEASURED on an NHANES paper: six of one run's twenty severity-bearing tool findings were this one error.",
